{
	// glass-hour at speed: the tune at its own height over a running
	// line. 26 bars, 44.98 s, five tempos stepping up and one step back.
	// f sharp phrygian, as glass-hour is. for @drive-low, which is
	// @analog-drive, a voicing the owner kept, at a lower master.
	//
	// the same idea as glass-acid, which the owner kept, turned the other
	// way up: there the tune is an octave down inside the running line;
	// here the lead plays glass-hour's own notes where glass-hour has
	// them, a bar of it to a bar of this at twice its tempo and rising,
	// and the running sixteenths are under it, in the bass, on the low f
	// sharp and its octave. it is held, as another way and not as a
	// correction: nobody knows which she prefers.
	//
	//   1-2   thin, 132 a minute. the bass line alone, with gaps, and a
	//         clock: a tick on each beat and a small metal tock between.
	//         bar 2 ends on g, the half step glass-hour leans on.
	//   3-6   ice. glass-hour bars 1 to 4 on the lead exactly as written
	//         (c# f# c# | d g d | c# f# c# | b e b) over the line on f#,
	//         g, f# and c, glass-hour's own bass notes.
	//   7-10  hairline, 136. a kick on every beat, the clock still
	//         ticking over it; the line loses its
	//         gaps and takes the shape of glass-hour's own bass at its
	//         crack (low low high low). glass-hour bars 5 to 7 on the
	//         lead as written. bar 10 is its silent bar with its snare
	//         roll: the line stops and the lead climbs the scale two
	//         octaves in sixteenths, quiet to loud.
	//   11-14 it spreads, 140 then 144 at bar 13. bars 5 to 7 again,
	//         every note of the tune now struck again on each sixteenth
	//         for as long as glass-hour holds it, the first of each
	//         loudest; chord stabs off the beat (the 64-bar piece's
	//         triads). the full drum: kick on every beat, a clap with it
	//         on two and four, an open hat between the beats. bar 14: the kick doubles, the lead leaps octaves
	//         up the scale.
	//   15-22 it breaks, 148, everything ff. glass-hour's crack: bar 9,
	//         each of its eighths struck twice, and bar 10, its run of
	//         sixteenths, exactly as written. then the 64-bar piece's two
	//         answers (its bars 35 and 36), the crack again, its rising
	//         run to the top d (bar 37), and bar 22 falls down the scale
	//         to one blow half way through the bar.
	//   23-26 after, back to 132. the clock, a held low note, and
	//         glass-hour's last bars as written: f# f# f#, the same on g,
	//         f# again, and it stops.
	//
	// accents: in the lead the first strike of each tune note is f or ff
	// and its repeats are not marked; in the bass the high note of each
	// group is one mark above the low ones. slides: the bass slides up
	// its octave once a bar; the lead slides only in the climb's first
	// four notes, the first four leaps of bar 14 and one turn in the run
	// (c# d c#). no note of the tune that begins a phrase is slid into,
	// because a note slid into is not struck.
